\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{One axis gives the second difference}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Take #{d=1} and #{N\ge 3}, with weight #{1} in both
directions. Then #{\omega=\gamma_1} and #{\omega^2=1}, so left
and right multiplication by #{\omega} agree: the algebra is
spanned by #{1} and #{e_1}, with #{e_1^2=1}. On a vertex
function, #{Df=(f(x+\varepsilon)-f(x))e_1}. Since #{\omega}
exchanges #{1} and #{e_1},
##{\omega Df=f(x+\varepsilon)-f(x),}
while #{D(\omega f)=f(x-\varepsilon)-f(x)}. The sum is
#{f(x+\varepsilon)+f(x-\varepsilon)-2f(x)}. Let #{f} equal
#{1} at one vertex and #{0} elsewhere. At the backward
neighbor the sum equals #{1}.}
